## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are highly porous, crystalline coordination polymers.[1] These regular molecular frameworks
As MOFs are both crystalline and porous, they have fascinating and often surprising properties. Not only does the immense chemical space spanned by these compounds create enormous potential for advancing materials science, it also provides ideal matrices for reference experiments exploring fundamental spectroscopic and physicochemical properties. The framework provides a well-defined, crystalline, porous environment that can host guest molecules, thus permitting systematic spectroscopic investigations. In contrast to solvents or polymer matrices, MOFs provide a strictly periodic, very well defined structural framework, where the molecular environment is identical for each embedded guest molecule. In this respect, MOFs outperform amorphous noble-gas guest matrices[5] in which the environment surrounding varies from site to site, leading to inhomogeneous broadening of the embedded guest’s spectroscopic features, including vibrational bands.
Herein, we discuss the use of MOFs and, in particular, of surface-mounted metal–organic frameworks (SURMOFs), to provide well-defined environments for precisely determining physicochemical properties. We especially investigate the specific activation energies for diffusion experiments and for the cisto-trans isomerization of a prototype photo­switchable molecule, azobenzene, and we examine the charge transport of electrons. In addition, we briefly highlight the huge potential of this class of materials for studying the coupling between chromophores; which leads to important changes in the optical properties of individual organic dyes in J-aggregates and H-aggregates, for example. Here, the specific advantages of SURMOFs will be highlighted. SURMOFs are thin, crystalline and highly ordered MOF films grown in layer-by-layer (LbL) manner (Figure 1) and are well suited for optical experiments. Their synthesis differs from the common solvothermal method for synthesizing MOF powders,[1b] as the reactants are not mixed and then heated but are kept separate. Well-defined, highly oriented MOF thin films are grown on appropriately functionalized substrates by subsequent immersion in different solutions containing the metal source and the organic ligands. This liquid-phase (quasi-)epitaxy not only permits well-defined electrical contacts to MOF-based materials to be fabricated, but also enables the realization of heteromultilayers. The organic–organic heterointerfaces within such structurally well-defined multilayers allow the study of the transfer of optical excitations and charge carriers across such interfaces.[6] Different LbL techniques, including spray coating,[7] spin coating,[8] and dip coating,[9] can be used for the SURMOF syntheses. For more details on the LbL synthesis of SURMOFs we refer to the recent literature.[10]
## 2. MOF Mechanical Properties
As MOFs have a well-defined, crystalline structure, their physical properties can be predicted using appropriate theoretical approaches. In previous work on HKUST-1 SURMOFs, standard indentation techniques were applied to determine Young’s modulus. The resulting experimental value, $9.3\ \mathrm{GPa}$ , was substantially higher than that for conventional organic poly­mers and it agrees well with theoretical results based on force-field calculations $(10\mathrm{GPa}^{[11]})$ .
Another interesting property of MOF materials is their negative thermal expansion coefficient. HKUST-1 SURMOFs on Si substrates (which exhibit a positive thermal expansion) were examined in out-of-plane and in-plane X-ray diffractometry under in situ heating. The resulting anisotropic expansion included an expansion $[\alpha_{\mathrm{{SURMOF,in-plane}}}=(11.7\pm3.7)\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{{K^{-1}}]}$ parallel to the substrate as well as a large contraction [αSURMOF,out-of-plane $=(-75\pm16)\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K^{-1}}]$ in the $z$ -direction, i.e., perpendicular to the surface.[12] An analysis of the behavior of this anisotropic thermal expansion for HKUST-1 MOFs was used to determine another important elastic constant of these crystalline molecular materials, the Poisson’s ratio, which had a value of $0.69\pm0.37$ . It is remarkable that HKUST-1 SURMOFs are highly stable when undergoing heating/cooling cycles, although there is a notable mismatch in the thermal expansion coefficients for the thin film and the substrate. This demonstrates that such coatings are stable and are well suited for applications in thermal-response devices. It should be noted that the experimentally determined elastic constants agree very well with computational values.
