Bochum University in 1997. In 2009, he became director of the Institute of Functional Interfaces (IFG) at Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT). His research focuses on surface science, including surface-supported metal–organic framework films.
## 3. MOF Thin Films with Very Low Defect Density
Many MOF thin film applications, such as sensors or optical devices, benefit from high structural quality combined with a low density of defects. Several cases demonstrated the substantially higher structural quality of SURMOFs compared to that of MOF powder-based materials obtained by conventional solvothermal synthesis. Such powders are also not well suited for optical applications or for thorough characterization of MOF optical absorption properties, since there is typically considerable scattering of light by nanometer- or micrometer-sized powder particles. In contrast, the scattering of thin SURMOF films is very small. In addition, SURMOFs with thicknesses below $100\mathrm{nm}$ usually show no charging effects under exposure to X-rays, so X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements can be performed in a straightforward fashion.
If the MOFs contain under-coordinated metal ions, then their defects can be characterized using infrared (IR) vibrational spectroscopy of small molecules adsorbed at such sites.
In the case of HKUST-1, CO molecules bound to $\mathrm{Cu^{++}}$ ions of the defect-free structure exhibit IR bands at $2179\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , whereas $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species, which result from defect formation, cause an ${\approx}60–\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ -shift to $2121~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .[13] Results from a combination of XPS and IR reflection absorption spectroscopy (IRRAS) demonstrated that HKUST-1 SURMOFs exhibit defect densities substantially lower than normal bulk samples. The density of the most common defect, missing linkers, can be as low as 3%–4%.[13]
Interestingly, in a different type of MOF—UHM-3, which is also formed from $\mathrm{Cu^{++}}$ dimers—the defect density was found to be much lower. XPS results revealed a $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ concentration below $1\%$ .[14] Heating the samples significantly increased the defect concentration (Figure 2a left). Monitoring the concentration of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ by recording the IR spectra of the CO molecules adsorbed at the Cu ions (Figure 2a right) shows identical results for the defect density.
In a later work, it was found that optimizing the conditions of LbL synthesis could significantly improve the quality of the SURMOF thin films. The surface roughness could be strongly reduced when using a dipping robot combined with an ultrasonic bath, as this results in more thorough rinsing of the sample surface during the synthesis. At the same time, the morphology becomes substantially more homogeneous, as demonstrated by atomic force microscopy and scanning electron microscopy.[9] In addition, as seen both visually and with UV–vis spectroscopy (Figure  2b), optical transparency is remarkably improved when employing the ultrasonic treatment during synthesis. While the samples prepared using the regular
LbL process appear as opaque turquoise, those prepared using the same reactants but with ultrasonication are optically clear with no surface scattering of light. Supported by detailed theoretical calculations and XPS data, the absorption in the visible range by the regular films was correlated to defects in the MOF structure, in particular to defects in the Cu-paddle-wheel unit where $\mathrm{Cu^{++}}$ is reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ .[15] The high-quality SURMOF films are suitable for various optical applications, including the thorough characterization of MOF optical properties. For example, a higher optical film quality enables the bandgap in HKUST-1 to be experimentally determined using ellipsometry.[16] Such investigations are difficult to perform for MOF powders, since scattering effects impede the collection of highquality UV–vis spectra.
The SURMOF defect density can also be controlled by using water as a modulator during the MOF growth[17] or by employing optimized synthesis parameters during the film formation.[18]
## 4. Determining Electrical Conductivity
Although MOFs were originally developed for applications in gas storage and separation,[1c] these highly flexible materials have attracted attention during recent years in quite different fields, including application as active and passive components in electronic devices.[19] However, to integrate MOF materials, the type of charge transport needs to be analyzed in the context of existing charge transport models, such as hopping, band-like transport or incoherent tunneling. Similar to determining MOF optical properties, MOF powders are poorly suited for thorough electrical characterization. The preparation of well-defined contacts to the electrodes makes it very challenging to collect reliable and reproducible data for particulate materials.
In this context, one type of MOF, HKUST-1, attracted considerable interest after Schlettwein, Wöll and co-workers[20] and later Allendorf and co-workers[19a,21] demonstrated that the conductivity of HKUST-1 SURMOFs, which in the pristine state behave as a large-bandgap insulator, can be strongly increased by loading electroactive molecules into the pores of the SURMOF. In case of the organic molecule tetracyanoquinodimethane (TCNQ), the gain in conductivity amounts to six orders of magnitude.[19a,21] These earlier findings were confirmed in later work by a sophisticated mercury-drop method to measure the current as a function of applied voltage (Figure 3a). This approach allows studying the thickness dependency of the HKUST-1 SURMOF conductivity in a systematic fashion. The resulting data demonstrated that the electric conductivity in the empty framework material can be described by incoherent charge hopping.[22] In the same study, the influence of ferrocene loading was investigated quantitatively (Figure 3b), and it was demonstrated that the conductivity increased by about an order of magnitude upon loading. Again, a charge transport mechanism involving incoherent tunneling and resulting in an approximately linear dependence of the resistivity on thickness was consistent with the data.
In contrast to the electrical properties of ferrocene-loaded HKUST-1, the high conductivity of the same MOF loaded with TCNQ was difficult to reconcile with conventional transport mechanisms. A detailed investigation revealed that both (simple) hopping and band transport models are inconsistent with the experimental data.[23] Detailed theoretical investigations revealed that the high conductivity observed in the experiment could be explained by an extended hopping transport model that includes virtual jumps through localized MOF states. This mechanism, also referred to as molecular superexchange, results in excellent agreement with the experimental data.[23a]
## 5. Unique Model System for Diffusion Experiments
For virtually all applications of nanoporous materials, such as zeolites, nanoporous carbons, and MOFs, the loading and diffusion of guest molecules within the voids are critical for governing the performance of these materials, e.g., as molecular containers or as membranes. Therefore, a detailed understanding of the diffusion processes is needed. Despite the substantial progress in obtaining fundamental insight into the diffusion and mass transfer of small molecules with relatively large diffusion coefficients in MOFs,[24] experimentally determining the low diffusion coefficients of large guest molecules remains a challenge. Pulsed-field gradient nuclear magnetic resonance and quasi-elastic neutron scattering, which are conventionally used to investigate diffusion in nanoporous materials,[25] are usually not suitable for determining diffusion coefficients smaller than $10^{-12}~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . The use of optical techniques, such as interference or infrared microscopy,[26] for determining small diffusion coefficients is hindered by their requirement for relatively large crystals of the nanoporous material $(\gtrapprox50\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ . Meeting this requirement leads to very long uptake and release times for slow diffusion processes. For example, it would take more than a year for a $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ MOF crystal to uptake molecules with a diffusion coefficient of $10^{-18}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . This problem can be overcome by using SURMOFs, where only one hour is needed for the uptake by a $100\mathrm{nm}$ thick film.[27]
Several previous works demonstrated that SURMOFs are ideal to serve as model systems for studying diffusion in nano­ porous frameworks (Figure 4). The uptake of guest molecules by a SURMOF, from either the gas phase or the liquid phase, can be easily studied using a quartz crystal microbalance (QCM). Growing the SURMOFs directly on the top Au electrode of the QCM sensor allows the change in mass of the MOF thin films to be straightforwardly monitored. Thus, it enables the amount of guest molecules that are loaded into the MOF pores to be quantified. Furthermore, the time dependence of the QCM signal can be used to investigate the mass transfer.[28] In addition to the ability to quantify the uptake amount and diffusion coefficient of guest molecules,[29] the activation energy for diffusion in MOF films can be determined through uptake experiments at different temperatures, thus allowing to compile an Arrhenius plot. In this way, the activation energy for ferrocene diffusion in SURMOF films of type $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})$ was found to be approximately $+90\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ (0.93 eV).[30] The rigid attachment of SURMOF samples to the solid support also permits investigations of the uptake from the liquid and gas phases by the very same sample.[31] Taking advantage of the optical transparency of high-quality SURMOFs allows the investigation of multicomponent, i.e., binary and ternary mixture, uptake of dye molecules.[32]
## 5.1. The Surface Barrier Phenomenon
After demonstrating the suitability of SURMOFs for studying diffusion in MOFs, we discuss the surface barrier phenomenon. This term is used to describe the additional resistances for the mass transfer of guest molecules in porous materials. In various experiments using large crystals of $\mathsf{M O F s}^{[33]}$ and zeolites,[34] it was shown that these porous materials possess additional mass transfer resistance at the external crystal surface, most likely resulting from near-surface structural imperfections of the porous matrix,[35] hindering guest molecules as they enter and leave the pore space. This apparently omnipresent mass transport barrier, present at the external surface of the porous particles, is usually referred to as a surface barrier. It varies widely for different measurements, even for the same powder material synthesized in subsequent batches.[34a] Due to these hindrances, measuring diffusion coefficients of molecules in MOF powders is highly problematic, and, for different samples of an identical material, diffusion coefficients are frequently reported that differ by several orders of magnitude. For example, reported experimental values of the diffusion coefficient for hydrogen in MOF-5 vary by four orders of magnitude, between $10^{-4}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1[36]}$ and $10^{-9}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ .[37] In order to resolve this issue and to determine intrinsic diffusion coefficients, thin, well-defined SURMOF films with an HKUST-1 structure were used to systematically study the surface barrier phenomenon in detail.[38] A QCM was used to investigate the uptake of a probe molecule, cyclohexane, by pristine HKUST-1 SURMOFs of different thicknesses, taking advantage of the capability to directly synthesize SURMOFs in the QCM cell without exposure to environmental conditions such as humid air. The sample mass, and thus the SURMOF thicknesses, were determined in situ by QCM during the synthesis. The experiments show an uptake time of cyclohexane that increases quadratically with the film thickness (Figure  5a).[38] This behavior is also predicted by Fick’s laws for pure diffusion-controlled uptake processes in a thin film (Figure  5c, left). Note that the presence of a surface barrier (Figure 5c, right) results in uptake times with linear thickness dependence (see Figure  5a). The quadratic increase in uptake time thus indicates that there is a negligible influence of the surface barriers on the molecule uptake of these pristine SURMOFs.[27]
Insight into the mechanism responsible for surface barrier formation were gained by experiments where pristine HKUST-1 SURMOFs were exposed to humid air or water vapor. The interaction with water slowed down the mass transfer (Figure  5b). XRD data[38] recorded after exposure showed that the bulk structure were unaffected by exposure to water vapor or humid air; thus, it can be concluded that the uptake process is hindered due to structural inhomogeneities at the surface. It was proposed that surface barriers were created when exposure to water damaged the MOF structure at the surface. Since a short exposure to humid air or water vapor is sufficient to cause surface defects, this hypothesis agrees with the omnipresence of these surface barriers in MOF materials. In a recent study, it was shown that the surface defects can be dissolved in ethanol at elevated temperature, resulting in a defect-free (outer) MOF surface and restoring the fast uptake and release of the guest molecules.[39]
## 6. Photoswitchable Nanoporous Coating
The integration of stimuli-responsive molecules, i.e., molecules that respond to external stimuli such as light, heat, electric fields, or changes in pH, into solids can be used to incorporate advanced properties in the material. A fairly large number of appropriate molecules are available and have been used to demonstrate the alteration of numerous molecular functionalities.[40] Since light is a simple and mostly nondestructive signal, optically active molecules with different conformations are of particular interest. An especially interesting and intensively investigated photoswitchable molecule is azobenzene, which has been used as a smart moiety for intriguing demonstrations of molecular functionality, including molecular machines such as “nanopliers”[41] or “nanocars.”[42] However, the integration of optical switches like azobenzene into solid materials remains challenging. If the molecular packing of azobenzene is too tight, e.g., in the crystalline bulk form, steric constraints prohibit light-induced switching between the trans and cis conformations. Nevertheless, intense research in this direction, mostly based on trial and error, has led to the identification of numerous successful cases, such as the incorporation of azobenzene in polymers and cross-linked liquid crystal polymers. For these amorphous materials, it was possible to demonstrate macroscopic, light-induced responses of macroscopic specimens resulting in changes in the length and shape[43] of samples made from this optically active material. The contraction and extension of the polymer have led to interesting applications, such as a photon-driven motor.[44] However, precise predictions and a direct transfer of the variation of the molecular properties, e.g., molecular length changes or dipole moment changes, to macroscopic variations of the materials are lacking to date. Due to the amorphous structure of these materials, a thorough quantitative analysis that allows a meaningful comparison with theoretical results has not yet been possible.
A different approach toward the realization of smart, lightresponsive materials is based on self-assembled monolayers, SAMs. In several cases, the switching of macroscopic properties, e.g., wettability,[45] was demonstrated. However, the effects are limited to the surface, and the influence of defects was demonstrated to be significant in many cases.[46]
For quantitative studies and a validation of theoretical results, there are striking advantages to use structurally welldefined, crystalline systems that contain sufficient free space to allow for conformational changes, such as MOFs and, in particular, SURMOFs. Azobenzene units can be incorporated as dangling side groups in the crystalline and nanoporous
MOF structure by attaching them to the organic linkers (Figure 6a).[47] The length of these linkers used in fabricating the MOF has to be adjusted so that the transition from the trans to the cis conformation is not hindered.[48] Therefore, photoswitching of a large fraction of azobenzene units is possible, resulting in physical and chemical material property changes of these optically active materials. Alternatively to the controlled incorporation of the photoswitches as dangling side groups to the MOF structure, the light-responsive mole­cules may also be embedded in the pores, resulting in straightforward preparation of photoswitchable nanoporous materials.[49]
## 6.1. Remote-Controlled Release of Guest Molecules from Nanoporous Films
Alkanes, alcohols, and diols were used as probe molecules to show that the switching effect, exemplified by difference in the loading by the trans and cis SURMOF, is caused by the switching of the azobenzene dipole moment, 0 Debye in trans and 3  Debye in cis, and dipole-dipole interaction (also known as Keesom interaction) with the guest molecules.[51] Comparing the change of the adsorption capacity caused by the trans–cis switching of the azobenzene pendant groups showed that a high density of azobenzene dangling groups per MOF unit cell volume increases the switching effect.[52]
## 6.2. Dynamic Remote-Control over the Membrane Permeation and Separation Factor
The use of nanoporous membranes to separate molecular mixtures is an attractive ecologic alternative to energy-intensive distillation and cryogenic separation processes. Due to their large variety and the ability to adjust their functionalization and pore size by modification of the building units, MOFs are promising materials for efficient membrane separation. Recently, the unique properties of these porous framework materials using membranes made from photoswitchable MOFs with azobenzene side groups was demonstrated for SURMOFs.[53] In these membranes, the separation selectivities could be varied over a wide range by illumination with light (Figure  6c). This approach used SURMOFs with a pillared-layer structure of type $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{AzoBPDC)_{2}(A z o B i P y B)}$ (AzoBiPyB: $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(2-(phenyldiazenyl)-1,4-phenylene)dipyridine (or dipyridylazobenzene)[48]). UV–vis and IR spectroscopy were used to investigate the light-induced isomerization of the azobenzene side groups. The cis-azobenzene ratio was switched between $0\%$ and $63\%$ , either with UV light (transto-cis) or blue light (cis-to-trans). Fine tuning of the cis-azobenzene ratio was enabled by short illumination times or by simultaneous irradiation with blue and UV light. The SURMOFs were deposited on mesoporous supports and used to investigate the separation of the binary ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{:}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ mixture. The photoswitchable membrane separation was measured using a Wicke–Kallenbach setup in which the membrane could be irradiated in situ.[53] The selection factor was reversibly switched between 3 and 8 by UV and blue light irradiation, i.e., by trans–cis switching the azobenzene side groups (Figure 6d). In addition, adjusting the cis azobenzene ratio between the minimum $(0\%)$ and the maximum $(63\%)$ values enabled the separation factor to be fine-tuned (Figure 6e). Thus, it is possible to continuously adjust the molecular composition of the permeate flux in a dynamic, remote-controlled fashion.
By using fluorinated azobenzene moieties,[54] photoswitchable MOF membranes can be prepared where the separation factor can be switched with visible light only, avoiding UV light.[55]
## 6.3. Photoswitchable Proton Conduction of Guest Molecules in Nanoporous Materials
Proton conduction, that is using protons instead of electron as charge carriers, in nanoporous materials attracts substantial attention with respect to applications in fuel cells, supercapacitors, chemical sensors, and information processing devices inspired by biological systems. A particular interesting goal is the dynamic remote-control over the proton conduction properties. To this end, SURMOFs with fluorinated azobenzene side groups,[55] which allow reversible light-induced trans–cis isomerization by visible light only, were used and the proton conductance of various guest molecules in the pores was investigated. The trans–cis photoisomerization results in the modulation of the interaction between MOF and guest molecules, 1,4-butanediol and 1,2,3-triazole; enabling the switching between the states with significantly increased (trans) and reduced (cis) conductivity, Figure 7. Quantum chemical calculations show that the trans-tocis isomerization results in the formation of stronger hydrogen bridges of the guest mole­cules with the azo groups, causing stronger bonding of the guest molecules and, as a result, a smaller proton conductivity.[56] The dynamic remote-control of the electronic conductance was recently demonstrated using photoresponsive spiropyran embedded in passive MOF films.[57]
## 6.4. SURMOFs as Regular Model System for Isolated Azobenzene Moieties
Apart from being functional coatings, azobenzene-containing SURMOFs can be valuable model systems for the determination of molecular properties. At room temperature, pure azobenzene $(\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{H}_{10}\mathrm{N}_{2})$ forms solid crystals, where the photoisomerization is sterically hindered.[58] In contrast, azobenzene photoswitching may occur in the gas phase,[5] in solutions or when integrated in polymers. There, the detailed dynamics, such as switching rate, energy barriers, and relaxation times, substantially depend on the local molecular surrounding.[59] For example, the relaxation rates in the dark from cis azobenzene to the thermodynamically stable trans form as well as the stability of the cis form in solution,[60] adsorbed in small pores[61] or integrated in polymers[62] varies considerably.
Large-pore SURMOFs with azobenzene pendant groups pointing into the pores of the framework and having no contact with other parts of the MOF can be considered as an exclusive model system to examine the isomerization of isolated azobenzene moieties, Figure  8a.[63] The trans-cis-isomerization was investigated at different temperatures in ultrahigh vacuum by IR vibrational spectroscopy, Figure  8b. Following UV irradiation, the rate constants of the thermal relaxation, i.e., the decay rate of cis azobenzene and the increase rate of trans azobenzene, in the dark were determined by analyzing the intensity of vibrational bands characteristic for the trans- or the cis-state. The linear slope in the Arrhenius plot, Figure 8c, enables the determination of the activation energy for the thermal cis-to-trans isomerization, $1.09\mathrm{~eV}\pm0.09\mathrm{~eV.}$ This value is in excellent agreement with the activation energy of isolated azobenzene molecules determined by quantum-chemical calculations.[64]
## 7. Transfer of Electronic Excitations across Organic/Organic-Heterojunctions
SURMOFs also offer the fascinating possibility of creating model systems for transport phenomena across organic–organic interfaces between two different compounds, A and B, including the transport of optical or electronic excitations. The epitaxial growth of B on A, yielding a well-defined heterointerface, can be realized via a layer-by-layer approach employing two different MOF types, A and B, that have similar crystal structures and linkers of the same lengths but different properties. With chromophore-based organic linkers, the transfer of optical excitation from one chromophore type to another can be studied. It is difficult to fabricate such well-defined interfaces for the normal bulk form of such chromophores, as they typically adopt rather different crystal structures. In addition, deposition of one layer of chromophore on top of a second requires the use of orthogonal solvents or of vapour phase deposition methods.
An early application of this approach studied the Dexter energy transfer of triplet excitons over organic–organic heterojunctions using the SURMOF approach. Two different chromophore-based linkers were used: Pd-DCP $({\mathrm{Pd}}({\mathrm{II}})5,15.$ diphenyl-10,20-di(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin) and ADB $^{(4,4^{\prime}}$ -(anthracene-9,10-diyl)dibenzoate).[6] This study demonstrated that these heterojunctions show solid-state photon upconversion (UC) transforming photons from green to blue (Figure  9). The structural property of the interfaces was intentionally varied between the ADB and Pd-DCP SURMOFs to demonstrate that the quality of the interface has substantial influence on the upconversion properties.
Since these heterojunctions are structurally well defined, they offer an excellent basis for comparison to theoretical results.
## 8. SURMOFs as Model-Systems to Study Intermolecular Interactions in Crystalline Chromophore Assemblies
The photophysical properties of chromophores are substantially affected by molecular packing and the surrounding. The interaction with neighboring photoactive species may substantially affect absorption and emission wavelengths, commonly referred to as solvatochromy (both, liquid and solid solvents). The most prominent examples are H- and J-aggregates, where chromophore assembly leads to dramatic changes of the properties of these photoactive molecules.[65] However, the precise structure of these aggregates formed by self-assembly in solution is often not known, severely hampering a more thorough understanding of the mechanism governing the chromophore– chromophore couplings. MOFs, and in particular SURMOFs, offer interesting options to fabricate crystalline chromophore assemblies, or CCAs, with well-defined structure, as well as high-quality CCA/CCA heterojunctions. A first example was the realization of crystalline H-aggregates, where drastic changes in the emission properties of anthracene-based systems could be monitored by using appropriate SURMOFs.[66] The monolithic structure of these CCA SURMOFs in particular allows for a detailed and precise determination of photophysical properties. In addition, transient absorption spectroscopy, TAS, can be applied in a straightforward fashion since SURMOF growth can be optimized to yield thin films of high optical quality.
In a more recent study, a SURMOF-based approach was used to realize highly anisotropic CCAs which exhibit directional energy transport.[67] In well-defined heteromultilayers, exciton diffusion could be shown to only proceed mainly along one crystallographic direction.
## 9. Summary
Herein, highly oriented, crystalline, thin SURMOF films are discussed with respect the application as model systems for studying molecular interactions in porous solids. In addition to phenomena related to diffusion—such as diffusion coefficients, activation energies and the importance of defects—other physical properties, such as electrical conduction mechanisms and optical properties, are discussed. The incorporation of photoresponsive molecules in the SURMOF structure results in nanoporous thin films with light-switchable physical and chemical properties, ideal for advanced applications. An emerging field are CCAs, crystalline chromophore assemblies, realized via the SURMOF approach, which can be used to realize anisotropic transport of (triplet) excitons.
